//             "<html lang=\"en\">\n<body>\n<p>Currently, research suggests that most people should sleep for between seven to nine hours each night. However, specific sleep needs do vary among individuals. Depending on your age and genetics, you might find you prefer seven, eight, or nine hours.</p>\n<p>While some might claim they feel fine getting less than six hours of sleep a night, it is likely they are simply used\n    to the negative effects of chronic sleep deprivation. Given how much research shows the importance of sleep, it's\n    100% in your best interest to increase your sleep to at least seven hours for a while and see how you feel.</p>\n\n<h2>10 ways to improve sleep</h2>\n\n<h3>1. Start the day in sunlight</h3>\n<p>Each morning, regardless if it's cloudy or not, go outside into natural sunlight for 15-30 minutes. This is one of\n    the best ways to set your internal clock to morning, and improve your ability to wake up.</p>\n\n<h3>2. Limit light exposure one to two hours before bed</h3>\n<p>Whether sunlight or artificial, light is a natural signal to your internal clock that it's not yet time to go to\n    sleep. Harsh screen lights in bed or right before bed confuse your internal clock and hurt your sleep.</p>\n<ul>\n    <li>Dimming lights to the lowest setting that still allows you to see in the evenings can help a lot.</li>\n    <li>You can also reduce the intensity of your phone's backlight and the time you use it at night.</li>\n    <li>When it's time to fall asleep, remove as much residual light as possible. For example, block out light from\n        windows with heavy blinds, and keep hallway lights off so they don't creep in under the door.\n    </li>\n</ul>\n<p>It's not all or nothing. Many small improvements to reduce your light exposure stack up and improve your sleep\n    quality.</p>\n\n<h3>3. Don't eat two to three hours before going to sleep</h3>\n<p>You don't digest food well when asleep. Eating large meals at night throws your internal clock out of sync and\n    significantly reduces the quality of your sleep. Ironically, the poor sleep may actually make you feel hungrier the\n    next day.</p>\n\n<h3>4. Exercise consistently ... </h3>\n<p>You may find there is a specific time of day when you enjoy working out because you seem to feel stronger and more\n    energetic. Listen to this feeling. It likely reflects your internal clock and when you are primed for physical\n    activity each day.</p>\n\n<h3>5. ... But not strenuously right before bed</h3>\n<p>Regular exercise improves sleep. In turn better sleep can improve muscle recovery from exercise. And bigger muscles\n    give you a bigger metabolism! However, rigorous exercise also increases your core body temperature, making it hard\n    to fall asleep afterward. Try to give yourself at least one hour after a workout before you go to bed.</p>\n\n<h3>6. Alcohol ruins your sleep quality</h3>\n<p>While alcohol may make you feel sleepy, it disrupts your sleep cycle during the night. So even if you fall asleep,\n    the quality is extremely poor.</p>\n\n<h3>7. Sleep in a slightly cool room</h3>\n<p>Your internal clock drops your core body temperature before bed, making you sleep better when the room is cool.\n    Studies show that this is typically between 68 and 72 degrees Fahrenheit. Experiment in this range and find which\n    temperature feels best to you.</p>\n\n<h3>8. Your bed is for sleeping</h3>\n<p>Your mind uses all sorts of cues to decide if it should sleep. When you consistently use your bed to watch TV, read,\n    or scroll through your phone, you confuse your mind as to whether it is time to go to sleep or not. When your bed is\n    only used for sleeping, it becomes a natural signal to your brain that it's time for sleep.</p>\n\n<h3>9. Use relaxation exercises</h3>\n<p>Stress and anxiety at night keep you awake, causing a cascade of signals that make you more alert and actively block\n    your ability to fall asleep. However, if you can engage your body's relaxation response, you can lower your blood\n    pressure, reduce your heart rate, and deepen your breathing, all of which help you enter sleep.</p>\n<p>You will learn about some specific relaxation exercises to use in future psychology lessons. Just be aware, these\n    practices typically don't work after one try. They should become part of your nightly routine, which can eventually\n    develop into further signals to your body that it's time for sleep.</p>\n\n<h3>10. Sleep when it feels natural to you</h3>\n<p>Some studies suggest that the best time for many people to go to sleep is between 10pm and 11pm. However, this does\n    not apply to everyone as it doesn't consider work schedules, family life, and genetic variations.</p>\n<p>What's more essential is that you identify the time that you feel naturally sleepy. This may change when you improve\n    your light exposure, bedroom environment, and don't eat just before bed.</p>\n<p>It's also always important to modify information like this to your own personal situation.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Overall, it's not about doing everything perfectly but about making manageable improvements that you can\n    maintain.</p>\n\n<p>Happy sleeping, and goodnight!</p>\n</body>\n</html>\n",
